WEBVTT

00:00:00.102 --> 00:00:05.787
This practice asks a conceptual question Why does
the null-safe filter

00:00:05.813 --> 00:00:10.866
return one extra row The ordinary condition says
payment status is not

00:00:10.892 --> 00:00:14.861
equal to Paid A null payment status cannot be proven
to be

00:00:14.887 --> 00:00:18.366
different from Paid so the comparison becomes
UNKNOWN and the row is

00:00:18.392 --> 00:00:24.526
removed The null-safe version says payment status IS
DISTINCT FROM Paid Now

00:00:24.552 --> 00:00:29.140
null can be included because PostgreSQL can make a
definite true-or-false decision

00:00:30.054 --> 00:00:34.926
Look for the record with a missing payment status
Then decide which

00:00:34.952 --> 00:00:39.193
business question you actually mean Do you want
records that are known

00:00:39.219 --> 00:00:42.956
to be something other than Paid Or do you want
records that

00:00:42.982 --> 00:00:48.021
are not definitely Paid Those are different
questions and they require different

00:00:48.034 --> 00:00:48.575
SQL
